     "id": "pp-caf1-gen-2-q8",
     "number": 8,
     "section": "B",
     "type": "NUMERICAL",
     "marks": 15.0,
     "text": "The following information relates to Coppervale Industries (CL).\n\nInformation:\n  At the reporting date the carrying amount of a non-current asset of Coppervale Industries (CL) was Rs. 3,900,000. The recoverable amount of the asset, being its fair value less costs of disposal, was Rs. 3,760,000. An impairment loss of Rs. 140,000 had been recognised in an earlier year and had not been reversed.\n  During the year the recoverable amount of the asset improved to Rs. 3,810,000.\n\nYou are given the following relationship:\n  An asset is impaired where its carrying amount exceeds its recoverable amount. The impairment loss is that excess. A reversal is recognised only to the extent the revised recoverable amount exceeds the carrying amount that would have existed had no impairment been recognised, and never beyond the original loss.\n\nRequired:\nDetermine whether the asset is impaired at the reporting date and calculate the impairment loss. Then calculate the reversal that may be recognised following the improvement in recoverable amount, and state the carrying amount after that reversal. Show all workings and state the limit on the reversal. (15 marks)\n\nNote to candidates: the relationship stated above is given. Marks are not awarded for reproducing it. You are required to show all workings and to state any assumption on which your answer depends.",
     "criteria": [
      {
       "id": "COMPARES-1",
       "label": "Compares the carrying amount with the recoverable amount and identifies that the asset is impaired",
       "maxMarks": 3.0,
       "evidence": "numericLines",
       "errorType": "CALCULATION_ERROR",
       "terms": [],
       "rule": "verb:compares"
      },
      {
       "id": "COMPUTES-1",
       "label": "Computes the impairment loss as the excess",
       "maxMarks": 2.0,
       "evidence": "numericLines",
       "errorType": "CALCULATION_ERROR",
       "terms": [],
       "rule": "verb:computes"
      },
      {
       "id": "IDENTIFIES-1",
       "label": "Identifies the limit on any reversal - the original loss, and the carrying amount that would have existed with no impairment",
       "maxMarks": 3.5,
       "evidence": "keyPointTerms",
       "errorType": "MISSING_POINT",
       "terms": [
        "limit",
        "reversal",
        "original",
        "loss",
        "carrying",
        "amount",
        "existed",
        "impairment"
       ],
       "rule": "verb:identifies"
      },
      {
       "id": "COMPUTES-2",
       "label": "Computes the reversal within that limit",
       "maxMarks": 2.5,
       "evidence": "numericLines",
       "errorType": "CALCULATION_ERROR",
       "terms": [],
       "rule": "verb:computes"
      },
      {
       "id": "COMPUTES-3",
       "label": "Computes the carrying amount after the reversal",
       "maxMarks": 2.0,
       "evidence": "numericLines",
       "errorType": "CALCULATION_ERROR",
       "terms": [],
       "rule": "verb:computes"
      },
      {
       "id": "PRESENTS-1",
       "label": "Presents the workings",
       "maxMarks": 2.0,
       "evidence": "structureTerms",
       "errorType": "PRESENTATION_ERROR",
       "terms": [],
       "rule": "verb:presents"
      }
     ],
     "expectedAnswer": "Impairment loss Rs. 140,000, being the excess of Rs. 3,900,000 over Rs. 3,760,000. Reversal recognised Rs. 140,000, limited by the original loss of Rs. 140,000; carrying amount after the reversal Rs. 3,900,000. The reversal is limited because a loss may not be increased beyond the amount originally recognised.",
     "markingNote": "",
     "workings": "Impairment loss = 3,900,000 - 3,760,000 = Rs. 140,000\nCarrying amount absent any impairment = 3,900,000 + 140,000 = Rs. 4,040,000\nMaximum reversal = 4,040,000 - 3,760,000 = Rs. 280,000\nBut the reversal may not exceed the original loss of Rs. 140,000, so the reversal recognised is Rs. 140,000\nCarrying amount after reversal = 3,760,000 + 140,000 = Rs. 3,900,000",
     "topicId": "caf1-ch4-t3",
